          ":question/id": "251cfe7e-474d-59b2-8ccf-101401daf068",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-139978",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,\u21d4\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,\u2713$ \n\nIndeed, since $det(A)=0,$ the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero.* $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn,  means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis so the dimension must be greater than zero.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,\u2713$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))\\ne 5,$ which means that $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \u201c$A$ has no inverse\u201d; \u201c$A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns\u201d only."
        },
